<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · song</p><h1 class="enc-title">Akathist Hymn</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">5th or early 6th century (formerly dated to the 6th or 7th)</span> · <a href="/centuries/6th-century">6th century</a> · Also known as Ἀκάθιστος Ὕμνος, Akathistos Hymnos</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Akathist Hymn takes its name from the Greek for &quot;unseated&quot;: while it is chanted, the congregation stands in reverence, and only the aged and the infirm sit. In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eastern-orthodox-church">Orthodox</a> and Byzantine Catholic services, where sitting, standing and bowing follow detailed rules, standing is required of all only for the Gospel, the Six Psalms of Matins and the akathists.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The Akathist is also known by the first words of its opening verse, Tēi hypermáchōi stratēgōi, &quot;To you, invincible champion&quot;, addressed to Mary. It is the akathist par excellence, written for the feast of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/annunciation">Annunciation</a> on 25 March. It was traditionally attributed to Romanos the Melodist, but recent scholarship rejects this, as it does for many other kontakia of the core repertoire.</p><p>Tradition links the feast to 626, when <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantinople">Constantinople</a>, under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/heraclius">Heraclius</a>, was attacked by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persians">Persians</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/avars">Avars</a> and was said to be saved through the intervention of the Theotokos. Sophocles disputed this origin, since the hymn could not have been composed in a day and its twenty-four oikoi contain no allusion to such an event. Authorship has also been given to Patriarch Sergius I of Constantinople and to George Pisida, but no certain ascription can be made. Under the influence of Leena Peltomaa's work, many scholars now favour a date in the 5th century or the early 6th.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Contents</h2><p>The Greek original consists of twenty-four oikoi, each beginning with the next letter of the alphabet, so that the hymn forms an acrostic. Twelve oikoi are long and twelve short. A long oikos has a seven-line stanza followed by six couplets that begin with the greeting Chaire, &quot;Rejoice&quot;, and end with the refrain &quot;Rejoice, Bride without bridegroom!&quot;. A short oikos ends with Alleluia. The series of greetings are called chairetismoi.</p><p>The hymn is divided into thirteen parts, each made up of a kontakion and an oikos. Its four sections correspond to the themes of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/annunciation">Annunciation</a>, the Nativity, Christ and the Theotokos herself. The final kontakion addresses Mary as the saviour of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantinople">Constantinople</a> in the siege of 626.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">How it was used</h2><p>Besides its dedication to the menaion, the Akathist had the political function of celebrating military victories and asking for divine protection in war. Since the 14th century it has belonged to the moveable cycle of the triodion, and the whole hymn is sung in four sections through Lent, in the service of the Salutations to the Theotokos on the first four Friday evenings of Great Lent. The entire hymn is sung on the fifth Friday, and the fifth Saturday, known as the Saturday of the Akathist, takes its name from it. In monasteries of the Athonite tradition it is often sung nightly at Compline.</p><p>Newer akathists to saints, mostly pastiches of the original, developed in the Slavic traditions. In the Greek, Arabic and Russian Old Rite traditions only the original may be used in formal liturgy. The Enchiridion Indulgentiarum of 2004 confirmed a plenary indulgence for reciting it piously. Three icons on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mount-athos">Mount Athos</a>, at Dionysiou, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hilandar">Hilandar</a> and Zographou, bear the title Akathist.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Akathist” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
